  "figure_descriptions": [
    {
      "element": "587:0",
      "role": "Figure",
      "page": 2,
      "alt": "Figure 1. A Jupyter notebook contains a code cell importing libraries and loading house-price data, a dataframe summary, a Markdown heading, and a histogram. A Share control enables a collaborative extension. The right panel illustrates the workflow: Bob enables sharing and sends a generated code; Alice joins with that code; both edit the notebook while it synchronizes changes and displays collaborators’ cursor positions and selections.",
      "actual_text": ""
    },
    {
      "element": "1461:0",
      "role": "Figure",
      "page": 19,
      "alt": "Figure 2. Paired participant timelines for six shared-notebook groups S1–S6 and six non-shared groups N1–N6 across four 15-minute sessions. Colored segments mark preparing, cleaning, modeling, feature engineering, and submission. Shared groups switch between phases more frequently and begin modeling earlier. Non-shared groups spend longer continuous stretches in cleaning or feature engineering before modeling. Brown submission segments appear mainly at the end.",
      "actual_text": ""
    },
    {
      "element": "1491:0",
      "role": "Figure",
      "page": 20,
      "alt": "Figure 3. Stacked response distributions on a seven-point scale from strongly agree to strongly disagree, comparing shared and non-shared notebooks. Combined strongly-agree and agree percentages, shared versus non-shared, are: following conversations 80% versus 79%; effective team communication 67% versus 77%; awareness of teammates’ subtasks 80% versus 71%; awareness of teammates’ technical issues 56% versus 40%; fair workload 70% versus 54%; all teammates fully engaged 77% versus 84%; enough work 82% versus 88%; waiting for others 14% versus 17%; enjoyment of teammates 82% versus 71%. Most responses are positive except the negatively worded waiting question, where disagreement is common. Bars also show somewhat agree, neutral and three levels of disagreement.",
      "actual_text": ""
    },
    {
      "element": "1561:0",
      "role": "Figure",
      "page": 22,
      "alt": "Figure 4. Group S3 notebook begins with a Pre-processing and cleaning plan: replace discrete values with indices; remove samples with too many missing features; normalize continuous variables; compute correlations or otherwise select features. Two code cells below implement count_nans, printing missing-value counts per column, and label_encoding, converting object-valued columns to categorical codes. Written subgoals coordinate the team’s later code work.",
      "actual_text": ""
    },
    {
      "element": "1571:0",
      "role": "Figure",
      "page": 23,
      "alt": "Fig. 5. An example of participants manually annotating a graph for discussion: P14 circled the outlier point\nin a scatter plot using MS Paint and sent it back to P15 using Slack.",
      "actual_text": ""
    }
  ],
